course: dictionary
lesson: 5759
title: Luckily and good luck
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Luckily is an adverb. It tells you more about how something happened. It means by good luck: something good happened by chance, and nobody planned it. «The door shut, but luckily her mum heard her knocking.» The girl was lucky that her mum was close enough to hear.
explain: Look for a problem followed by a happy turn. Luckily often starts a sentence to show that things got better. «Luckily, the dog was found.» Before this, something was probably wrong, and luckily tells you that good news came next.
explain: You can also use the phrases luckily for me and luckily enough. «Luckily for me, I had my coat.» Both mean that good luck helped. When luckily starts a sentence, put a comma after it.
fact: Luckily means by good luck, or it is lucky that...
fact: «Luckily» often starts a sentence, and a comma follows it: «Luckily, the dog was found.»
fact: Luckily usually links a problem to a good result: first something went wrong, then good luck helped.
fact: Luckily is an adverb, but lucky is an adjective: a lucky boy, luckily he was found.
fact: «Luckily for me» and «luckily enough» are useful phrases to remember.
exercise: «Luckily, the dog was found.» What does luckily mean here? | Very slowly | *By good luck | By a loud noise | Angrily
exercise: «The door shut, and she could not open it from inside, but luckily her mum heard her knocking.» What does luckily show? | *Good luck helped the girl get out | The mum was angry with her | The door was painted red | The girl had a bad day
exercise: Which sentence uses the comma correctly? | Luckily the, boy found his mum. | Luckily, the boy, found his mum. | *Luckily, the boy found his mum. | Luckily the boy found his mum.
test: «Luckily his primary head also wrote that Jake had improved a lot.» What does luckily mean here? | Sadly and slowly | In a rude way | Very quietly | *By good luck
test: Mia says luckily is the same kind of word as lucky. Is she right? | Yes, they mean exactly the same thing in every sentence | No, luckily is a noun | *No, lucky is an adjective and luckily is an adverb | Yes, they are both adjectives
exam: In «His mum helped him contact the shop with the receipt, and luckily they found the card again.», what was the good luck? | The receipt was lost for ever | Nobody could help them | The shop was closed | *They found the card again
